hostname r3
!
ip routing
!
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! r3 — Internal Router in Area 0 (backbone)
!
! r3 is a normal OSPF router entirely within area 0.
! It has no direct knowledge of the NSSA mechanism — it simply
! receives Type-5 LSAs from r2 (who translated them from Type-7)
! and installs them as O E2 external routes.
!
! This is the key point of NSSA: the external prefix originates as
! Type-7 inside area 1, but area 0 routers like r3 see it as a
! normal Type-5 (AS External) LSA. From r3's perspective, it looks
! exactly like a route redistributed by a normal ASBR.
!
! Ethernet1: connects to r2 (10.1.23.0/30, r3=.2) — OSPF Area 0
! Loopback0:   loopback 10.0.0.3/32
! -------------------------------------------------------------------

interface Loopback0
 ip address 10.0.0.3/32

interface Ethernet1
 no switchport
 description to-r2 (OSPF area 0)
 ip address 10.1.23.2/30

! -------------------------------------------------------------------
! TASK: Configure OSPF on r3
!
! router ospf 1
!  router-id 10.0.0.3
!  network 10.0.0.3/32 area 0
!  network 10.1.23.0/30 area 0
!
! r3 does NOT configure NSSA — area 1 NSSA is only relevant to
! routers inside area 1 and the ABR.
!
! After full configuration, verify on r3:
!
!   show ip ospf neighbor
!     -> should see r2
!
!   show ip ospf database external
!     -> should see a Type-5 LSA for 192.168.100.0/30
!     -> originated by r2 (router-id 10.0.0.2) — it's the translator
!
!   show ip route ospf
!     -> O E2 192.168.100.0/30 [110/20] via 10.1.23.1  <- external route
!     -> O    10.1.12.0/30 [110/20]  <- intra-area/inter-area from r1
!
!   ping 192.168.100.2 source 10.0.0.3
!     -> should succeed (ext node is reachable via r1)
!
! Compare Type-5 (seen on r3) vs Type-7 (seen on r1):
!   On r1: show ip ospf database nssa-external
!   On r3: show ip ospf database external
!   Note: Type-7 uses different P/F/T bits and has forward address
! -------------------------------------------------------------------
